exec(open('/tmp/security04-helper.py').read());n='web-sop-cors'
qr(n,2,['prompt'],'What happens?','Assume no CSP or other sending restriction and a reachable server. What does CORS itself do?','Separate CORS permission from other sending policies and server behaviour.')
q(n,2,['options',1,'text'],'The simple request can be sent, but the script cannot read the response without CORS permission','Server processing and successful side effects are not guaranteed.')
q(n,2,['options',1,'explanation'],'Correct. This simple request needs no preflight. The server may accept or reject it; absent CORS permission the response is not exposed. Default fetch credentials mode omits cross-origin cookies.','Original implied authenticated side effect despite default cookie omission.')
q(n,2,['workedExample'],'The string body gives a safelisted text/plain content type, and this POST has no unsafe author-set headers. Under the stated assumptions the browser sends it without preflight, but default cross-origin fetch does not include bank cookies. The server decides whether any action is authorised. Without allow-origin permission the CORS fetch rejects; it does not expose response contents. CSRF still matters for requests that carry eligible ambient credentials or otherwise cause an unauthorised action.','Distinguish request delivery,authentication,side effects and response sharing.')
q(n,3,['options',3,'explanation'],'Classic no-cors embedding may work, but CORS-enabled images, module scripts and other resource modes can require CORS approval. Embedding also depends on CSP, CORP/COEP and framing policy.','Embedding/CORS modes not universal.')
q(n,3,['workedExample'],'CORS grants browser response-sharing permission and controls preflight approval for non-safelisted requests. Simple requests can still be sent without permission to read the response, while failed required preflights can prevent the actual request. Non-browser clients are not bound by browser CORS enforcement. The server must authenticate and authorise independently.','Remove internally contradictory neverrestricts claim.')
q(n,4,['prompt'],'A SPA makes CORS fetches to another origin, with no cached preflight permission. Assume safelisted header values where named, no extra author-set headers, no streaming body or upload-listener trigger, and no other sending restriction. Which need an OPTIONS preflight? Select all that apply.','Header names alone and warm preflight cache made original always claim false.')
q(n,4,['options',0,'text'],'GET /items with only Accept: application/json','Provide explicit safelisted value.')
q(n,4,['options',2,'explanation'],'Correct under the supplied empty-cache conditions. DELETE is not a CORS-safelisted method.','Cached permission can avoid OPTIONS.')
q(n,4,['workedExample'],'Under these assumptions, GET with a safelisted Accept value and POST with a safelisted multipart content type avoid preflight. JSON content type, DELETE and the Authorization header require permission and therefore OPTIONS with an empty preflight cache. Header lengths/bytes and API-specific flags matter too; a header name alone is not the complete safelist test.','Complete safelist constraints and cache scope.')
q(n,5,['options',1,'explanation'],'Credentials mode include does not guarantee any eligible cookie exists or is sent. The wildcard fails the CORS credentials-mode check regardless; stripping cookies from an already received response is not the rule.','Credentials mode is decisive,not actual cookiepresence.')
q(n,5,['workedExample'],'With credentials mode include, the CORS check cannot use wildcard allow-origin, even when no cookie is eligible. An allowed named origin plus Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true is required to expose the response. Eligible cookies remain subject to SameSite and browser policy. For dynamically selected origins preserve Vary: Origin, and separately prevent shared caching of private user data.','Credentials-mode semantics and cache privacy.')
q(n,6,['prompt'],'An API reflects origins for which the Python suffix check below succeeds, and sends Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true. Assume the attacker controls the candidate origin and eligible victim credentials are sent. Which untrusted domain passes?\n\n```python\ndef allowed(origin):\n    return origin.endswith(\n        "example.com")\n```','Do not assert real domain availability or guaranteed credential delivery.')
q(n,6,['options',1,'explanation'],'Correct. The suffix comparison accepts that string although its hostname is not example.com or its subdomain. The question assumes attacker control; actual domain registration availability is irrelevant.','Remove assertion anyonecanregister actualdomain.')
q(n,7,['options',1,'text'],"The cache may reuse origin A's allow-origin header for origin B, causing B's CORS check to fail",'MissingVary alone doesnotmake originAheaderauthorizeB.')
q(n,7,['options',1,'explanation'],'Correct if the shared cache does not otherwise vary on Origin. Its key may include other dimensions; omission does not imply URL-only caching.','Cache policy can vary independently.')
q(n,7,['workedExample'],'A cache stores a response authorising app.example and reuses it for admin.example because Origin is not distinguished. Admin’s browser rejects the mismatched header. Vary: Origin enables correct variation in conforming caches when the response depends on Origin; preserve other Vary values too. It does not fix unsafe shared caching of user-specific content: use appropriate Cache-Control and authentication-aware cache policy. Correctly allowlisting origins plus missing Vary does not by itself authorise an untrusted origin.','Remove unsupported cross-origin data exposure claim and distinguish usercacheprivacy.')
q(n,8,['options',1,'explanation'],'Correct as a trust-boundary flaw: distinct opaque origins serialize as null. Exposure of authenticated data additionally requires eligible credentials, suitable CORS credentials permission and a sensitive response.','Null is a serialization,not one shared opaqueorigin; exploit prerequisites.')
q(n,8,['workedExample'],'A sandboxed iframe with allow-scripts but without allow-same-origin can execute with an opaque origin serialized as null. This gives an attacker a matching origin value if the API allowlists null. Reading private data additionally depends on credential eligibility, request mode and endpoint behaviour. Do not use null as the identity of a trusted support tool; serve it from a controlled named origin.','No unconditional bypass of thirdpartycookiepolicy.')
q(n,9,['options',2,'explanation'],'Correct. Also verify the expected source window when applicable and validate the payload structure. An untrusted window with a reference can send messages; checking origin alone does not make arbitrary data safe.','Origin-only validation insufficient andnotanywindowhasreference.')
qr(n,10,['prompt'],'What is the real impact?','Assume a logged-in victim triggers the XSS, eligible API cookies are sent, and the API responds with sensitive data. What becomes possible?','Supply exploit prerequisites instead of implying universal cookie delivery.')
q(n,10,['options',1,'explanation'],'Correct under the supplied conditions. Trusting the compromised origin grants access to the API responses that its credentials and authorisation permit, not automatically every resource.','Avoid full APIread universal.')
q(n,10,['options',2,'explanation'],'The stated HTTPS sibling origins are same-site in an ordinary first-party context. SameSite alone therefore does not isolate them; cookie domain/path/expiry and other browser policy still matter.','SameSitecontext and other eligibility constraints.')
q(n,10,['workedExample'],'The injected script runs on the allowed promo origin, requests the account API with credentials mode include, and receives the sensitive response under the supplied cookie and server assumptions. CORS permits reading it. Narrow exact-origin allowlists and removal of unused subdomains reduce this trust surface; fixing XSS and server authorisation remain necessary.','Eliminate unconditional cookie/completeAPIclaims.')
q(n,11,['prompt'],'Assume a Chromium build whose preflight-cache maximum is 7,200 seconds. A successful preflight advertises Access-Control-Max-Age: 86400. What upper expiry bound follows, allowing earlier eviction?','Make evolving implementation parameter explicit; not guaranteedbrowserretention.')
q(n,11,['options',0,'explanation'],'The supplied implementation cap is lower than the advertised value, so86400seconds is not the upper expiry bound.','Remove unverified currentFirefoxcomparison.')
q(n,11,['options',1,'explanation'],'Correct. min(86400,7200)=7200 seconds, or two hours. Entries can be evicted sooner.','Retentionnotguaranteed.')
q(n,11,['workedExample'],'The supplied cap gives min(86400,7200)=7200seconds. Fetch permits an implementation cap and earlier removal; absent or invalid max-age defaults to5seconds in the standard. The checked Chromium source uses two hours, but future versions and other browsers require their own checks. A cached permission avoids an extra preflight only when its key and method/header conditions match.','Version-specific source-backed bound instead ofalwayscacheexactly.')
coverage(n)
